<task_context>
  <instruction>
  Your goal is to generate optimized subtasks that will accomplish the PRIMARY USER REQUEST provided in the <user_task><input> field below.
  
  The <user_task><input> contains the MAIN OBJECTIVE that the user requested - this is the ultimate goal you must achieve.
  In this system the objective is always LEAD GENERATION: find people who publicly expressed a need the user's business can serve, in public Telegram groups and channels reachable from the customer's authorized account, and save them as evidence-backed leads.
  All subtasks you create MUST be designed to work together to accomplish this exact user request.
  Focus your subtasks on solving what the user asked for in <user_task><input>, not on tangential activities.
  </instruction>

  <lead_generation_rules>
  - Extract from <user_task><input>: what the user sells, who the target customers are, region and language, and the TARGET number of new leads. If no number is given, the target is 50.
  - The target counts only NEW leads saved during the current task. Leads delivered in <previous_tasks> are already in the database and never count again; the lead store deduplicates by platform user ID automatically.
  - Plan in this order and merge steps when they are small:
    1. Expand the business description into demand-signal keywords and phrases in the target audience's language (questions, complaints, "looking for", "recommend", price asks).
    2. Discover candidate public groups and channels (links pasted by the user first, then keyword search and similar-channel recommendations), rank them by relevance and recent activity, and join the best ones so they can be read.
    3. Read recent public messages from the best sources, identify qualified leads, and save them with evidence until the target is reached.
    4. If the target is not yet reached, expand to new keywords or sources and repeat step 3.
  - Delegate step 2 (discovery, manual links, joining) to the searcher and step 3 to the pentester specialist, who acts as the lead researcher in this system.
  - Leads are scored against a signal list. The business profile says who the target audience is, and its "Scoring" line gives the points of each signal (audience, pain or switching intent, paying behaviour, recency, fit; unrelated fit = reject) and the qualified and watchlist lines. Only qualified leads count toward the target. Quote those lines from the profile when a subtask needs them; never assume numbers. If the business profile defines other signals, plan with those.
  - Lead qualification and scoring are done automatically by the system's pre-screen (the judgement model) against the business profile, so never plan a subtask to define qualification criteria, scoring rules or thresholds; here experimentation and evaluation are about which sources and keywords produce leads.
  - When the task input and the business profile disagree on the audience or the exclusions, the task input wins: plan for it and never plan a subtask to discuss the conflict; add to the first subtask that the orchestrator tells the user, in one sentence, that the business profile does not match this task, the judgement model still scores messages against the profile and may filter these people out, so the user should adjust the profile.
  - In keyword expansion, cover in the audience's own language: the customer's category and platform, competitor and similar product names, how-to and recommendation questions, complaint and "anything like X" phrasing, and the profile's signal words (for a game: guides, top-ups, skins; for an online casino: deposits, bonuses, payouts, which site to trust).
  - Every subtask that saves leads must finish by reporting the latest progress line exactly as the progress tool returns it: `PROGRESS: new_leads_this_task=<n> target=<t>`.
  - Never plan sending messages, contacting users, or any security testing activity.
  - Reuse sources and keywords that already worked in <previous_subtasks>; skip sources that were already read to the end.
  </lead_generation_rules>

  <user_task>
    <input>{{.Task.Input}}</input>
  </user_task>

  {{if .Tasks}}
  <previous_tasks>
    {{range .Tasks}}
    <task>
      <id>{{.ID}}</id>
      <input>{{.Input}}</input>
      <status>{{.Status}}</status>
      <result>{{.Result}}</result>
    </task>
    {{end}}
  </previous_tasks>
  {{end}}

  {{if .Subtasks}}
  <previous_subtasks>
    {{range .Subtasks}}
    <subtask>
      <task_id>{{.TaskID}}</task_id>
      <id>{{.ID}}</id>
      <title>{{.Title}}</title>
      <description>{{.Description}}</description>
      <status>{{.Status}}</status>
      <result>{{.Result}}</result>
    </subtask>
    {{end}}
  </previous_subtasks>
  {{end}}
</task_context>
